  "description": "A quick win: understand Sound Localization: The Duplex Theory.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "A car honks on your left and you flinch away instantly. Your brain pinpointed that sound in under one millisecond.",
      "visual": "🚨"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Sound Localization sets up the neural system, the mechanism, and why it matters.",
      "visual": "📐"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "High-frequency sounds are blocked by the head, so the brain compares loudness.",
      "visual": "📊"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "The medial superior olive processes time differences with microsecond accuracy using neurons that act like coincidence detectors.",
      "visual": "⏱️"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "The lateral superior olive processes level differences and combines them with time data to build a full spatial map of sound.",
      "visual": "🗺️"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Your brain runs two localization systems at once, one for timing and one for volume.",
      "visual": "🔀"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What does the duplex theory of sound localization describe?",
    "options": [
      "Using time differences for low frequencies and level differences for high frequencies",
      "Using only one ear to detect all sounds",
      "Relying on vision to locate sounds"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
